    {
      "question": "q-2545",
      "field": null,
      "attribute": "question/worked-solution",
      "old_sha256": "9963e7d51e95dff3cf6d7cdc3e17038f08219dc47e72e0220c15fe4c3e879d96",
      "new_sha256": "c25e45aab39901511bfb6a0e07b495d6eeb57889210f094936ae841e1b268817",
      "category": "ma_capture",
      "authoring": [],
      "source": [
        {
          "question": "q-2545",
          "field": null,
          "attribute": "question/worked-solution",
          "value": "The area of a circle is given by\n\n$$\nA=\\pi {r}^{2},\n$$\n\nwhere $r$ is the radius of the circle.\n\nWe know that $C=2\\pi r.$ so we can express the area $A$ as a function of the circumference $C$ by substituting $r=\\frac{C}{2\\pi }$ in the formula above. We get\n\n$$\nA=\\pi {r}^{2}=\\pi {(\\frac{C}{2\\pi })}^{2}=\\frac{{C}^{2}}{4\\pi }.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the rate of change of $A(C)$ with respect to $C$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{\u2032}(C) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}C}(\\frac{{C}^{2}}{4\\pi }) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4\\pi }\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}C}({C}^{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4\\pi }(2C) \\\\  & =\\frac{C}{2\\pi }.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
          "category": "ma_capture",
          "file": "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14037675/state.json",
          "file_sha256": "d4e5a48a7258fe67eaecb883db90efba2089bb6b04e5d48438a775866113552b"
        }
